{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve for \\( P(16) \\), we start by analyzing the given inequalities for the quadratic polynomial \\( P(x) \\):\n\n\\[ x^2 - 2x + 2 \\le P(x) \\le 2x^2 - 4x + 3. \\]\n\nSince \\( P(x) \\) is a quadratic polynomial, we can express it in the form \\( P(x) = ax^2 + bx + c \\). The inequalities suggest that \\( P(x) \\) is squeezed between two quadratic polynomials. To find \\( P(x) \\), we will determine its coefficients \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\).\n\nFirst, we check the values of the bounding polynomials at \\( x = 1 \\):\n\n\\[ 1^2 - 2 \\cdot 1 + 2 = 1 - 2 + 2 = 1, \\]\n\\[ 2 \\cdot 1^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 + 3 = 2 - 4 + 3 = 1. \\]\n\nSo, \\( x^2 - 2x + 2 \\) and \\( 2x^2 - 4x + 3 \\) both equal 1 at \\( x = 1 \\). This suggests that \\( P(1) = 1 \\). Next, we check the values at \\( x = 2 \\):\n\n\\[ 2^2 - 2 \\cdot 2 + 2 = 4 - 4 + 2 = 2, \\]\n\\[ 2 \\cdot 2^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 + 3 = 8 - 8 + 3 = 3. \\]\n\nSo, \\( x^2 - 2x + 2 \\) and \\( 2x^2 - 4x + 3 \\) are 2 and 3 respectively at \\( x = 2 \\). This suggests that \\( P(2) \\) should be between 2 and 3. We can use these points to form a system of equations for \\( P(x) \\). Since \\( P(x) \\) is a quadratic polynomial, we can write:\n\n\\[ P(x) = ax^2 + bx + c. \\]\n\nFrom \\( P(1) = 1 \\), we have:\n\n\\[ a(1)^2 + b(1) + c = 1 \\implies a + b + c = 1. \\]\n\n","prefix_end":1241,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,369,17767,393,7,16,21,8,1124,701,582,1191,553,41018,279,2661,92234,369,279,79151,47311,17767,393,2075,8,1124,7731,78045,856,61,17,481,220,17,87,488,220,17,1124,273,393,2075,8,1124,273,220,17,87,61,17,481,220,19,87,488,220,18,13,1124,2533,12549,17767,393,2075,8,1124,8,374,264,79151,47311,11,582,646,3158,432,304,279,1352,17767,393,2075,8,284,3859,61,17,488,44241,488,272,1124,568,576,92234,4190,429,17767,393,2075,8,1124,8,374,64162,1948,1378,791